          "text": "The user asks about run_case.cpp implementation approach. This is a design/learning question. We're in plan mode, read-only. She's asking:\n\n1. Is reading input file like this OK?\n2. How to open stdout/stderr files for create/overwrite (CREATE_ALWAYS)?\n3. In the while loop, should we accumulate into a structure or feed to Main.exe process on the fly?\n\nKey conceptual point: The approach she's sketching — reading the input file with std::ifstream, reading lines, and then... presumably writing to the child process — is a WRONG mental model. In variant A, run_case does NOT read the input file itself. The whole point is: CreateProcess with handle inheritance — we pass the *handle* of the input file as the child's stdin. The OS streams the file to the child; run_case doesn't touch the bytes.\n\nSimilarly stdout/stderr files: we open them (CreateFile with CREATE_ALWAYS), and give handles to the child as hStdOutput/hStdError. The child writes into them directly. run_case doesn't read child output and write to files.\n\nThis is a really important teaching moment. Her sketch (ifstream + getline loop) suggests she's thinking \"run_case reads input lines and forwards them to the process\" — which is the pipe/stdio-forwarding model, NOT the file-handle redirection model. That model would be needed if stdin came from somewhere run_case had to mediate, but here since it's a file, we just hand the file handle over.\n\nSo answer:\n1. Not OK for the intended design — reading the file in C++ is unnecessary. The file handle is given to the child. std::ifstream would read the file into run_case's memory — pointless, and then you'd have to write bytes into the child's stdin pipe — that's a whole different (more complex) architecture with pipes and I/O multiplexing.\n2. CREATE_ALWAYS — that's a Windows API flag, so we're not using std::ofstream; we use CreateFileW (Windows API) returning HANDLE. For files: input CreateFileW with GENERIC_READ, OPEN_EXISTING; stdout/stderr CreateFileW GENERIC_WRITE, CREATE_ALWAYS, FILE_SHARE_READ maybe. Set bInheritHandle via SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES.\n3. On the fly vs structure: neither — because run_case doesn't pass data at all; it passes handles. That's precisely what makes it simple and shell-free: the OS does the streaming.\n\nBut wait — there's subtlety. Should she read lines to detect errors? No. run_case's job is just exit code. Content checking is separate (check tool). So run_case never looks at content.\n\nHowever, there's an alternative simpler C++ approach that might tempt: use <cstdio> freopen? Actually freopen on stdin wouldn't redirect child. Or _dup2 + _spawn... Actually on Windows CRT there is `_spawn` family. But we settled on CreateProcess.\n\nSo we should make the key architectural clarification: distinguishing two models:\n- Model 1 (what she sketched): run_case reads input, and must feed child via pipe → needs CreatePipe, write loop, careful about deadlock if child's stdout fills pipe buffer while we write stdin. Complex.\n- Model 2 (chosen variant A): file handle → child's std handles directly. No data flows through run_case. Simpler, no deadlock.\n\nSince input is a file (not interactive), Model 2 works and is the point.\n\nLet me answer the three questions concisely but pedagogically, since she's learning. Give the mental model shift. Provide correct API sketch (not full code, since plan mode and she writes code). Explain CREATE_ALWAYS flag belongs to CreateFileW not std::ofstream.\n\nAlso note: to answer whether to read whole input into structure — if she were in a model where she forwards, she'd need on-the-fly with pipe, but not structure. But really the answer is: neither, because the OS redirects.\n\nAlso cross-platform note for later Linux: dup2 approach mirrors this — child process has fd 0 replaced by open file.\n\nLet me frame with [ФАКТ] etc per her AGENTS rules. Keep concise but this deserves some explanation. Language: Russian.\n\nI should be careful: she explicitly asked \"ответь только на данный вопрос\" earlier for a previous one; this time no such constraint. So answer fully.\n\nLet me give precise API names on Windows:\n- Input: `CreateFileW(stdin_path, GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ, &sa_inherit, OPEN_EXISTING, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, NULL)`\n- Output: `CreateFileW(out_path, GENERIC_WRITE, FILE_SHARE_READ, &sa_inherit, CREATE_ALWAYS, FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL, NULL)` — CREATE_ALWAYS truncates/creates.\n- SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES with bInheritHandle=TRUE.\n- STARTUPINFO: STARTF_USESTDHANDLES, hStdInput, hStdOutput, hStdError.\n- CreateProcessW(..., TRUE /* inherit */, ...).\n- Close handles in parent after spawn (child has its own copies). Actually parent should close its copies of the handles after CreateProcess, so that when child exits... well for wait we need process handle not file handles. We can close file handles in parent right after CreateProcess since child inherited duplicates.\n\nFor wide vs narrow strings: CreateProcessW needs wide strings. argv comes as char*, need conversion. Could use CreateProcessA to keep char* simplicity? CreateProcessA exists. But MSVC toolchain compiles; using A variant is simpler for char*. But encoding of paths... source dir path ascii mostly. Might mention use CreateProcessA for simplicity given char* argv, or convert to wchar. Keep it as a note.\n\nI'll answer the three subquestions and the deeper point.",
