          "text": "The user asks about the SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES with bInheritHandle=TRUE. Questions:\n1. Is it done before CreateFileW?\n2. Is it Windows-specific?\n3. Show how to do it.\n\nThis is a learning question about Windows API. Let me answer concisely and show code. This is plan mode but answering a conceptual question with a code snippet in chat is fine — I'm not editing files.\n\nLet me explain:\n\nSECURITY_ATTRIBUTES is a Windows struct. Yes it must be set before CreateFileW because you pass a pointer to it as the second parameter of CreateFileW (lpSecurityAttributes). Yes, it's Windows-specific. On Linux, the analogous concept is handled differently — fork/dup2 doesn't need inheritance flags because child inherits file descriptors by default after fork (unless FD_CLOEXEC).\n\nLet me show:\n\n```cpp\nSECURITY_ATTRIBUTES sa{};\nsa.nLength = sizeof(sa);\nsa.bInheritHandle = TRUE;\nsa.lpSecurityDescriptor = nullptr;\n```\n\nAnd then pass `&sa` to CreateFileW.\n\nThe struct definition:\n```cpp\ntypedef struct _SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES {\n  DWORD  nLength;\n  LPVOID lpSecurityDescriptor;\n  BOOL   bInheritHandle;\n} SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES;\n```\n\nYes, must be before each CreateFileW call that needs inheritable handle. The bInheritHandle field determines whether the returned handle can be inherited by child processes.\n\nAlternative: CreateFile also has flag FILE_FLAG... no. There's also the approach of using SetHandleInformation to set HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT on the handle after creation.\n\nLet me be concise but pedagogical, in Russian.",
