handle to the same file object? When inheriting a handle, the child's handle refers to same file object, sharing the file pointer offset. Since parent never writes, fine.\n\nSo CloseHandle(in/out/err) after successful CreateProcess — correct and recommended. Show syntax: `CloseHandle(in); CloseHandle(out); CloseHandle(err);` or one line each. Note CloseHandle returns BOOL, can check but usually ignore.\n\nBut wait — in the failure branch `if (!ok)` we return 1; if we close handles only after CreateProcess success, then in failure case handles in/out/err leak — but process exits immediately anyway (return 1), OS reclaims. Fine.\n\nActually also err/out handles opened before CreateProcess; if CreateFileW fails (returns INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE), she doesn't check. Should check each CreateFileW for INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE with diagnostics. Currently not checking — worth noting.\n\n8. Line 82: `si` declared STARTUPINFOW but we need STARTUPINFOA (if CreateProcessA). Also should set si before.\n\n9. Missing `<windows.h>` — includes commented out. This is the big one.\n\n10. `if (argc < 7)` check: minimum argv count. Let's count: argv[0]=prog, argv[1]=code, argv[2]=input, argv[3]=stdout, argv[4]=stderr, argv[5]=\"--\", argv[6]=exe. That's argc==7 minimum. So argc < 7 → error. Correct. Good.\n\n11. She checks argv[5]==\"--\". Good.\n\n12. expected_code: argv[1] parsed? Not yet. Not used yet. She'll parse with stoi probably. That's later code to add (wait + compare). Not present.\n\n13. Also note: she does the file opening with CreateFileW but then has #ifdef _WIN32 only around handle creation and CreateProcessA part... Actually #ifdef starts at line 59 and there's no #endif. Lines: 59 `#ifdef _WIN32`, ... through end of file line 93 — no `#endif` seen. Let me check: after line 90 return 1, line 92 return 0, line 93 `}`. No `#endif`. So missing `#endif`. Also on non-Windows, everything inside #ifdef is compiled out, including `return 0;`? Wait the `return 0;` at line 92 and closing brace line 93 — if #ifdef _WIN32 wraps through end, on Linux main would have nothing and fall through end without return → compile warning/error (main returns int, no return → UB, but with flags error maybe). Missing #endif → unterminated conditional → compile error! Actually preprocessor: #ifdef without #endif at EOF is error \"unterminated #ifdef\". So yes must add #endif. And need #else branch (Linux later) with at least return 1 \"not implemented\". For now she's Windows-first; so #endif after the Windows code plus a placeholder #else return 1... But then main body structure needs care. Plan: add #endif after Windows code and #else with print \"Linux not implemented\" return 1.\n\nActually careful: main function overall has stuff before #ifdef: argc check and \"--\" check are platform-neutral, fine. Then Windows-specific part. Then #endif then #else... The #else would need to attach to the same #ifdef — meaning the Linux branch would skip everything between #ifdef..#endif. That's the whole body after the checks. So:\n\n```\nif (argc<7) ... return 1;\nif (argv[5]!=\"--\") ... return 1;\n#ifdef _WIN32\n   ...windows stuff...\n   return 0;\n#else\n   print \"not implemented\"; return 1;\n#endif\n```\n\nBut currently `return 0;` sits after; needs to be inside Windows branch. So restructure with #else.\n\nActually the plan-mode answer: I shouldn't edit. Present findings: what to add/fix, and answer CloseHandle question.\n\nLet me also double check CreateFileA vs CreateFileW given the choice of CreateProcessA. Mixing W for file create and A for process — inconsistent but each could compile individually if strings converted. But she passes std::string to CreateFileW → won't compile. Recommend CreateFileA for consistency with char* argv, or keep everything W with conversion. Given the earlier conversation she was leaning CreateProcessA for simplicity. So recommend CreateFileA too. Note FILE_SHARE_READ for out/err might be questionable — sharing read while we write; child inherits handle; sharing flags affect whether OTHER processes can open; fine.\n\nAnother: cmdline quoting of args with spaces not handled for argv[7..]; noted before.\n\nAlso `#include <system_error>` missing.\n\nAlso mixing: SA bInheritHandle = true (bool) vs TRUE — fine but consistent style; BOOL field; true converts.\n\nAlso CreateFileW requires wchar_t; so either way.\n\nAlso potential: `CreateFileA` path is char*, okay.\n\nAlso note: handles in/out/err must be checked for INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE.\n\nAlso after we set si before building handles, ordering fine.\n\nWait: cmdline uses argv[6] path as lpApplicationName too. lpCommandLine must start with argv[0] which we quote path. Fine.\n\nLet me also mention: `PROCESS_INFORMATION pi{};` needs declaration and zero-init.\n\nNow for the CloseHandle question: Yes — close parent's in/out/err after successful CreateProcess (child holds its own inherited copies). Show syntax:\n\n```cpp\nCloseHandle(in);\nCloseHandle(out);\nCloseHandle(err);\n```\n\nPlacement: after ok check passes, before WaitForSingleObject. Actually you can close right after CreateProcess returns TRUE. Child has duplicates already. That's the recommended time.\n\nThen continue: WaitForSingleObject(pi.hProcess, INFINITE); GetExitCodeProcess(pi.hProcess, &exit_code); compare; CloseHandle(pi.hProcess); CloseHandle(pi.hThread);\n\nLet me answer concisely but thoroughly. Also note run_case now uses ON? In tests/CMakeLists line 2 configure_target(run_case OFF). Fine.\n\nAlso the file currently has encoding issues (mojibake) — but that's the console read; ignore.\n\nAlso note missing #endif is compile error, missing pi, missing ; on line 82, wrong type si (W vs A), CreateFileW+narrow string mismatch, missing windows.h. Also the comment lines 6-8 show windows.h commented → must uncomment/include.\n\nLet me write plan-mode answer as review + guidance + the code for CloseHandle + next steps. Since plan mode active, do not edit. Provide the answer.\n\nI'll structure findings as a numbered list with categories.",
