ions where wrong diagnostic for a branch.\n\nThe user says non-emptiness check is meaningless — she's right. The meaningful check is the exact expected message (contains/equal). So the question becomes: for cases that were \"code-only\", should we upgrade to also check stderr content?\n\nMy honest recommendation: exit-code-only tests are legitimate when the test's purpose is the code contract (the machine channel). stderr content testing duplicates the diagnostic text and creates brittleness — every wording tweak breaks tests. But §4.6 says diagnostics matter to the mentor. Yet the mentor reviews messages; tests pinning exact Russian strings make refactoring wording painful. \n\nA balanced answer: keep code-only cases as code-only initially (parity with make), but for each task at least ONE representative test per error branch should check stderr via --contains to catch \"message went to stdout instead of stderr\" or completely wrong text. Actually check tool compares files: run_case already separates stdout and stderr into two files. A key risk: diagnostics printed to stdout instead of stderr would be caught by code-only tests? No — code still right, stdout would contain the message. If we never check stdout emptiness for error cases, a message leaking to stdout wouldn't be caught... but the acceptance in make? Task policy §4.9 p7: tests check all three channels. For error cases expected: stdout empty.\n\nHmm. Let me keep answer concise. She asked \"что ты посоветуешь\" — give a recommendation with reasoning, weigh tradeoff. Recommend: don't add blanket content checks. For code-only: check only code (parity + less brittleness). But add a targeted minimal guard that stdout file is empty for error-branch code-only cases? That catches message-to-wrong-channel regressions without pinning wording. Actually that's cheap and meaningful: run_case already produces out file; an --equal against empty file? check --equal against an empty expected file would verify stdout empty