es it print before error? Let me not assume.\n- t3: bbox - stores points, on error returns before output. From journal similar to t4.\n- t4: fixed on 08-28 to store-then-print. stdout empty on error.\n\nBut wait - is checking \"stdout empty on error\" even that meaningful given exit code is already checked? The exit code distinguishes the error branch. If stdout had partial data but exit code was correct, would we catch it? Only if we check stdout is empty. Is it worth it? \n\nThe user's argument: the value is low, err might contain junk, checking emptiness is meaningless. For stdout, emptiness on error is a meaningful design invariant (pipeline safety). But is it worth a test? It's a judgment call.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. What does the acceptance test want to verify? Per § 4.9 p7: \"Тесты проверяют все три канала: stdout (рабочий вывод), stderr (диагностика — не глушить), exit-код\". So the policy says tests should check all three channels! stdout for error case = expected empty. This is actually per course policy.\n\nHmm but for \"code-only\" cases, in make they dropped stderr to null and didn't check. Under § 4.9 p7, testing all three channels means for an error case you'd check stdout empty (it's deterministic), stderr non-empty or contains expected substring, exit code. But that may be over-testing. The user wants pragmatic.\n\nLet me not over-engineer. Concede the mischaracterization. Then reframe: the actual question is whether we pin \"stdout empty on error\" invariant. Give her honest assessment:\n\n- Her core point stands: stderr diagnostics don't leak to stdout; in the 08-27 case both streams went to the same console because no redirection. Correct.\n- The real 08-27 defect was different: stdout emitted a point before discovering the error — partial output. That was fixed on 08-28 by storing points.\n- So if we want a test that protects that specific fix, we'd check stdout empty on error branch. But is it worth it? Since exit code already tells th