          "text": "The user asks a conceptual question: what's bad about losing output when there's no console under ctest? Or is it technically fine, but an antipattern?\n\nLet me answer clearly. Under ctest, the child inherits run_case's stdout/stderr if we don't redirect. Actually, wait — in our design we always redirect to files. The question is hypothetical: if we didn't create out/err files, child would inherit run_case's console. Under ctest there's no console, so output would be \"lost\". What's wrong with losing output?\n\nAnswer: For code-only tests, nothing is strictly wrong — you only care about the exit code. But: \n1. CTest itself captures the test process's stdout/stderr into logs (LastTest.log / output). If run_case doesn't create files but child inherits run_case's streams, then run_case's own stdout/stderr go to ctest which captures them. Actually, if run_case doesn't redirect child's handles, child writes into the inherited console handles → which under ctest are... CTest runs tests with stdout/stderr piped to its log files by default (unless --output-on-failure and passed through). So output isn't really \"lost\" — it lands in ctest's log. But the point: we can't distinguish child's stdout from run_case's own diagnostics, and we can't feed it to `check`. So \"losing\" here means: not available as a file to compare later.\n\nThe real reasons files always:\n- Determinism/visibility: if the child outputs to the shared inherited stream, run_case's own messages and child output interleave in one stream, corrupting both.\n- The exit-code test is cheap; but to compare content (check) you need a file. One uniform interface.\n- ctest without --output-on-failure swallows output; debugging a failed code-only test would show nothing about why child failed.\n\nAlso there's technical subtlety: If you don't set hStdOutput/hStdError and don't set STARTF_USESTDHANDLES, child inherits run_case's console. Under ctest there typically IS no console (ctest itself run from console but... actually ctest launched from PowerShell console does have a console). Under CI/no console, CreateProcess for console app may fail or allocate a new console window (a visible window popping up!) — that's a real bad thing: CREATE_NEW_CONSOLE by default for console subsystem apps when parent has no console. That would flash windows. So losing = not exactly; rather you'd get pop-up consoles or no console errors.\n\nHmm, careful. Let me be accurate: When a process with console subsystem is created and the parent doesn't have a console... CreateProcess allocates a new console unless DETACHED_PROCESS or CREATE_NO_WINDOW. So child might pop a console window. That's a genuine technical problem, not just antipattern.\n\nBut honestly the user's deeper question: is it ok to discard output for code-only tests? Answer: discarding child output is fine in principle (you only check exit code). But implement \"discard\" explicitly by pointing handles at the null device (NUL), not by leaving them unset/inherited. Leaving them unset is the antipattern because:\n- interleaves streams, \n- may allocate console windows,\n- removes debuggability.\n\nGiven our design already always creates files — simplest and uniform; then code-only tests just don't run check. Could alternatively point to NUL to avoid disk writes, but then files are gone. Keep files always for uniform interface + possible debugging.\n\nAlso note: even in \"code-only\" test, the out/err files contain the diagnostic text; if test fails on code, you can read err file to see why. That's valuable.\n\nKeep answer concise. Plan mode - read-only, no edits. Just answer conceptually.",
