          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me analyze the question. She asks: \"Do I correctly understand that the logic now is: if it's an erroneous test, we check via --contains, and if correct data — via --equals. Then is there any point passing the check flag in add_case if it's all so deterministic?\"\n\nLet me think about this carefully. Her claim: error cases → --contains, success cases → --equals. Is that actually true/deterministic?\n\nLet's think about the semantics:\n- --contains: expected is a substring of result. Used for error message fragments.\n- --equals: full equality after CRLF normalization.\n\nIs it deterministic that error → contains and success → equals? Not necessarily. Consider:\n- t2_norm (success full output) → --equals. Yes.\n- error cases → the message printed is short; could be checked with --equals too, if the whole stderr is just that message. Actually --equals on the whole stderr file would also work if stderr contains exactly the message (plus possibly nothing else). But stderr might have extra warnings, so --contains is more robust for error diagnostics.\n\nBut is success always --equals? Consider success on stdout — full output comparison → --equals. That's the norm case.\n\nBut actually there's nuance. The distinction she's drawing is really: whether we compare the *whole stream* or just *search a phrase*. The mode depends not on \"error vs success\" per se but on whether we have a full canonical expectation or a fragment.\n\nIs the mapping error→contains truly universal in her tasks? Let me think about t2. t2 reads name and vertex count. Error cases: empty name, EOF... diagnostics. Success: \"Фигура ...: N вершин\". For t2 success output is full stdout → equals. For t4 radius errors (arg parsing) → error in stderr → contains of message fragment.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: error messages can also be compared fully by --equals if stderr contains nothing else. But diagnostics messages end with values — the expected text may contain the actual parsed input. So --contains of a phrase is natural.\n\nBut wait, is the deterministic mapping really \"error → contains\"? Consider that in her code, error diagnostics to stderr contain the value (e.g., \"Получено: %s\"). To compare equals, you'd need to reconstruct the exact string including the echoed input value. That's brittle. So --contains of a fixed phrase prefix is more natural for error paths. And --equals for success path stdout full compare.\n\nHowever — is that universally so? Could there be a success case checked with --contains? Possibly checking stdout contains some value. Could there be an error checked with --equals? If stderr is deterministic and short. Not necessarily universal.\n\nAlso, there's a real subtlety: the choice between --contains and --equals isn't merely \"error vs success\". The real axis is: do we expect the *entire* stream to match a canonical file, or only a phrase to be present? Success full-output tests usually want exact equality (regression guard — any change to output fails). Error tests usually only need to verify the diagnostic message (we don't want to hard-code entire stderr which might have ordering or extra content, and messages include runtime values like line numbers echoed input).\n\nActually wait — but error tests: could they use --equals with full expected stderr file? For t1 error cases, stderr is just one message + \"\\n\"? Let me recall. Actually in her err.txt the message appears. Hmm.\n\nBut the real point of her question: if mode is fully determined by whether it's error or success, why pass mode as a parameter to add_case at all? She could derive it.\n\nLet me think whether the mapping is truly deterministic. If YES and always, then indeed the `mode` param is redundant and she should remove it (derive contains vs equals from expected exit code != 0 or from which stream). If NO — there are exceptions — then mode must stay.\n\nI think the honest answer: the mapping is NOT fully deterministic and there are real counterexamples in her own test set, so mode (or an equivalent) is still needed. Let me find concrete cases.\n\nConsider the distinction between --contains and --equals as \"full document equality\" vs \"fragment search\".\n\nLet's think of counterexamples:\n1. Success case with full-output equality (t1_test1, t2_norm) → equals on stdout. Deterministic \"success → equals\" holds here.\n2. But what about a success case where output has floating numbers with many digits that could vary? They compare formatted 3 decimals, so equals is fine.\n3. Error case t1: on bad input, stderr has message \"N должно быть...\" Actually her t1 error messages: \"N должно быть целым числом. Получено: %s\" — contains the value. To use --equals you'd need exact reconstruction. So error→contains natural.\n\nBut consider an error case where we want to assert the *full* stderr is exactly the message and nothing more (no stray output). contains wouldn't catch extra junk. So sometimes equals on error is desired for stricter verification.\n\nAlso consider: A successful run's stderr — might be empty. Do we check success stderr is empty? Possibly not needed.\n\nActually more fundamental counterexample: whether to check stdout or stderr. For error cases we check stderr. For success cases we check stdout. So which *file* to compare is determined by exit code. But within a stream, contains vs equals is about \"fragment vs whole\".\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether error→contains is a rule she actually can rely on. Consider task 2 error path: vertexes out of range. The diagnostic is like... she'd check that stderr contains \"вершин\"? Not necessarily full.\n\nBut is there any error case in her suite where equals would be needed/appropriate over contains? equals is stricter; contains is weaker. equals could always replace a \"must be exactly this\" scenario. The pragmatic question: do we ever have an error where contains is insufficient? \n\nThe real determinant of contains vs equals is: **how much of the stream is deterministic/canonical**. For success output that we fully generate (matrix, report) → whole stream canonical → equals natural and stricter (catches extra/missing). For diagnostics that embed runtime values (echoed bad line, line number) → only the fixed phrase is stable → contains natural.\n\nBut it's NOT purely \"error vs success\": e.g., in success t4 case, the passing-point lines depend on input and radius; the expected file is fully determined by input — canonical → equals. In success t1, matrix is deterministic → equals.\n\nNow, could there be a \"success\" check that uses contains? E.g., a success test where we only verify the output begins with / contains something, because full output has variable parts we don't want to fix? In their geometry, outputs are deterministic formatted — so no need.\n\nCould there be an error test needing equals? Consider error where stderr should be exactly one message and we want to assert no stray diagnostics. Using contains, extra junk would pass silently — that's the \"мусор в err\" concern she raised earlier. So there IS a legitimate use of equals on error: to assert the diagnostics is exactly the expected message (stderr file == expected error file). Actually check.cpp --equal normalizes CRLF and compares whole file.\n\nBut here's the thing: their err file for an error test contains exactly the single diagnostic line (program prints one message then returns). So equals against an expected error-file would work and be stricter. Why did make tests use contains (findstr) instead? Because of encoding issues / partial matching convenience. Hmm. Actually check on stderr for errors: the message echoes the bad input value, which in the expected file is known (input fixed in test) — so full equality is reconstructable. So actually error→equals is also feasible!\n\nSo is mode truly derivable? The axis really is:\n- stdout full-result tests → equals\n- stderr diagnostic tests → could be contains (robust to embedded values) OR equals (stricter, full message)\n\nNot cleanly \"error→contains, success→equals\". There are design choices.\n\nI should give an honest nuanced answer. And note the earlier principle: keep mode explicit rather than implicitly derived from exit code because:\n1. exit code != 0 doesn't uniquely determine \"contains on stderr\"; could be equals-on-stderr or no-content-check-at-all (code-only).\n2. Deriving behavior implicitly makes the CMake function less explicit — magic rules that a reader must reverse-engineer; parameter makes call sites self-documenting.\n3. She earlier decided code-only cases (no content check). So expected_result empty/mode empty is a real third state.\n\nActually she decided code-only cases stay code-only (only exit code). So add_case has at least states: (code only), (check contains), (check equals). If mode were derived from exit code, you couldn't represent \"code-only error case\" vs \"error with contains check\" — both exit nonzero. Wait — in her current tests, code-only cases and content cases: she decided to ADD stderr checks to t1-t2 error cases (the \"дыра\" discovery). So error cases will now have contains checks. But will ALL error cases get contains? Maybe. And will success cases always get equals? She has t1_test1, t2_norm equals. What about t3/t4 success — equals on stdout. Probably.\n\nBut even if today every error→contains and success→equals, encoding that as implicit rule in the function is fragile: tomorrow she adds a test where error stderr should be equals-strict, and the function can't express it without a mode param. Better to keep parameter explicit.\n\nHmm — but she's asking \"is there a point in passing the flag if it's deterministic?\" Perhaps the deeper question is about YAGNI / not passing redundant args. Honest answer: the mapping is *mostly* but not *strictly* deterministic, and more importantly, mode encodes intent at the call site. If she finds mode always one value per exit-code-sign, she could drop it — but then add_case signature loses expressiveness, and there's at least one axis not captured: which *stream* (stdout vs stderr) — that's arguably the more important discriminator, and it IS determined by exit code (error→stderr, success→stdout). So maybe the parametrization she really needs is \"which file to check + full-or-fragment\", which could be collapsed.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: could she make add_case auto: if expected code != 0 → check stderr contains expected_result; else → check stdout equals expected_result. If expected_result empty → code only. Is there a real counterexample where this fails in her suite?\n\nCounterexample attempt: t2 success case checks stdout equals (norm). Error t2 checks stderr contains. OK. t3 success → stdout equals. t3 error → stderr contains of message. t4 success → stdout equals. Radius error → stderr contains. Seems consistent! So maybe she's actually right and it IS deterministic in her suite.\n\nBut there's a subtle case: what about tests that check the SUCCESS case has nothing on stderr, or checks stdout on an \"error\"? Hmm, error exit but stdout should be empty — we decided not to check that. So fine.\n\nBut wait, t1 matrix: for wrong N like N=2 (out of range) → error → stderr message → contains. t1 float N → \"N должно быть целым\" contains. t1 abc → contains. Consistent.\n\nWhat about \"expected code == 0 but we also want to ensure stderr empty\"? Not needed.\n\nAnother subtle case: what if a success case ALSO writes to stderr some warning, and we want contains on stderr? Not in her suite.\n\nSo honestly: within the CURRENT suite, exit-code-sign might deterministically imply (stream, full-or-fragment). But:\n1. It's a hidden coupling — reading add_case call, you see expected_result and code, and must infer the mode from context rules. A reader (mentor!) has to know \"error ⇒ contains on stderr\". That's implicit knowledge. The AGENTS file emphasizes explicitness and diagnostic clarity; implicit magic in a build function is the kind of thing that bites later.\n2. Robustness to future: stage 2/3/4 evolve tasks; new tests may legitimately need equals on stderr (strict full-message) or contains on stdout, and then add_case without mode can't express it without restructuring.\n3. It conflates two orthogonal axes: (a) error/success (which exit code carries anyway), (b) full-vs-fragment comparison. Even if correlated today, they're different decisions.\n\nCounter-argument (her side): fewer params = simpler calls; \"mode\" duplicates info already in exit code; if it's truly redundant today, passing it invites inconsistency (someone passes contains for a success case, check passes stdout equals but flag says contains → nonsense). Hmm, actually there IS a real hazard in the other direction: two independent params (expected_excode and mode) can contradict, and the function must enforce the invariant. If mode is derived, contradiction impossible.\n\nSo it's a genuine design tension. My job: lay it out, give recommendation.\n\nMy honest recommendation: I lean toward keeping a small explicit discriminator, but reframe it around what actually varies — the *target stream and comparison*, not an error/success \"mode\". Actually simplest and cleanest given her suite: derive default but allow override? Overengineering.\n\nLet me think about what mode really needs to express. Possible comparisons she may want:\n- stdout equals full (success)\n- stderr contains phrase (error)\n- (maybe) stdout empty on error — she decided NO.\n- code-only — no content check.\n\nSo the function could be: add_case(name exe args input expected_code expected_result) where:\n- if expected_result empty → code-only\n- else if expected_code != 0 → check stderr --contains expected_result\n- else → check stdout --equals expected_result\n\nThat removes mode entirely and encodes current rules. Risk: hidden coupling, future rigidity.\n\nOR keep mode but make it meaningful: mode ∈ {contains_stderr, equals_stdout, none} — but that duplicates the derivation.\n\nHmm, there's actually a cleaner way to think: what varies across her calls is genuinely just expected_result and expected_code. The mode is *derivable*. So per DRY/KISS, deriving is defensible. But explicitness...\n\nI think the honest answer is: she's partially right — today it looks deterministic, and if she's confident it always will be, she can drop mode. BUT the determinism claim \"error→contains, success→equals\" is a *heuristic over her current suite*, not a law, and there's a better discriminator underneath. I should probe whether she has cases that break it, and recommend keeping the parameter but redefining it OR document the derivation.\n\nActually, I want to push back on the premise gently: the real discriminator is not \"error vs success\" — it's which stream and full/fragment. But wait, for error cases stderr is a single deterministic message (could be equals too). So even error-vs-success doesn't force contains. She chose contains for errors probably out of robustness. So there's an inconsistency available: an error test could be equals if the full message is canonical.\n\nLet me give a crisp answer with the tension and a recommendation, and ask a clarifying question: does she foresee ever wanting equals on stderr or contains on stdout? If never → derive and drop mode. If possible → keep explicit.\n\nI must not edit files (plan mode). Provide reasoning. Should be fairly concise but this is a design discussion. Let me also verify by looking at her actual expect files to ground claims? There might be t2 error expects? Let me quickly check tests/expect contents mapping — but I can't fully determine which are stdout/stderr from names alone. Could inspect a couple: t1_nul is error (stderr) expectation. t1_test1 — success stdout. t2_norm — success.\n\nI could check whether any expect file corresponds to error-with-equals. Not necessary; reasoning stands. But to be accurate about \"deterministic in current suite\", I could note both. Keep it read-only — fine.\n\nLet me also mention: contains vs equals has a \"strictness\" axis: equals on stderr would be stronger. And the check that errors write NOTHING to stdout was declined. So the function, if derived, is fine.\n\nI'll answer with the tension and ask the clarifying question.\n\nKeep it focused. Not too long.",
