          "text": "Let me analyze what I see.\n\nIn the root CMakeLists.txt:\n```cmake\nadd_case(t1_c_empty $<TARGET_FILE:t1_c> \"\" t1_empty 66 CONTAINS t1_empty 2)\n```\n\nSignature: `add_case(test_name exe args input expected_excode check_flag expected_result stream)`\n\nMapping:\n- test_name = t1_c_empty\n- exe = $<TARGET_FILE:t1_c>\n- args = \"\"\n- input = t1_empty\n- expected_excode = 66\n- check_flag = CONTAINS\n- expected_result = t1_empty\n- stream = 2\n\nIssues:\n\n1. **exe should NOT be `$<TARGET_FILE:t1_c>`.** Inside the function, exe is used in `$<TARGET_FILE:${exe}>`. If we pass `$<TARGET_FILE:t1_c>` as exe, then inside it becomes `$<TARGET_FILE:$<TARGET_FILE:t1_c>>` — nested generator expression which is wrong. The add_case function already wraps exe in `$<TARGET_FILE:${exe}>`. So the caller should pass just the target name `t1_c`, not the generator expression. That's a bug.\n\n2. The `exe` param is at position 2. Wait the add_test in function: `$<TARGET_FILE:${exe}>`. So passing exe as generator expression double-wraps.\n\n3. Variable name mismatch `PROGRAMM_RESULT` (typo for PROGRAM_RESULT) - internal, consistent though.\n\n4. In input path: function uses `${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/input_data/${input}`. The caller passes `t1_empty`. But there's no `t1_empty` in input_data! The glob shows input_data has t1_test1, t1_nul, t1_low, t1_high, t1_float, t1_abc. No t1_empty. The expect has t1_empty though (new file exists). Wait glob: input_data does NOT have t1_empty; expect DOES have t1_empty. Hmm. So input file name mismatch: they intend empty input. Previously they used t1_nul for empty input file in tests. Actually t1_nul is the empty file (nul = null). They renamed the expected to t1_empty in expect (newly created), but the input file t1_empty doesn't exist in input_data. Actually wait, in the earlier version the add_test used input_data/t1_nul and expect/t1_nul. Now root CMakeLists uses input t1_empty and expect t1_empty. But glob shows input_data