run_case argv after `--`: `$<TARGET_FILE:${exe}>` then empty string. run_case parses argv[6] as exe; empty argv[7]. Then cmdline builder loops from 7 to argc. It would append empty. cmdline gets an extra space. Child gets argv possibly empty argument. Could be ok but messy. Better approach: build the args into the list conditionally. Also they have TODO comment L41 noting that.\n\nActually empty string `\"\"` argument: CMake generator might drop it? Not certain. Messy; note it.\n\n7. The if/else at L19-23 has logic bug:\n```\nif(NOT ${check_flag} STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\" AND NOT ${check_flag} STREQUAL \"EQ\")\n  message(FATAL_ERROR \"unknown flag...\")\nelse(NOT ${stream} EQUAL 1 AND NOT ${stream} EQUAL 2)\n  message(FATAL_ERROR \"unknown stream...\")\nendif()\n```\nProblems:\n- `else(...)` with a condition is allowed syntax in CMake but the condition is a diagnostic only (since CMake 3.8 it must match for error). Actually else(condition) is allowed; but semantics: else block executes when if condition false. Putting a different condition inside else() parentheses is meaningless/misleading. It does NOT re-test. So this will FATAL_ERROR \"unknown stream\" whenever check_flag IS valid (else branch runs when check_flag condition is false, i.e. when check_flag is valid → it prints unknown stream error!). That's a logic bug: the second FATAL will always fire when first doesn't.\n\nIndeed: if check_flag is invalid → first if true → fatal unknown flag. If check_flag valid → else branch → fatal \"unknown stream\" — WRONG. So any valid call fails at configuration.\n\nAlso these two are independent validations that should be two separate ifs, not if/else.\n\nAlso using `if(NOT ${check_flag} STREQUAL ...)` — dereferencing variable without quotes; if check_flag empty → `if(NOT  STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\")` weird. Should quote: `if(NOT check_flag STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\" ...)`. Actually `if(NOT \"${check_flag}\" STREQUAL ...)`. Better use variable directly: `if(NOT \"${check_flag}\" STREQU