nknown stream...)` REGARDLESS of whether stream is valid — because else always runs when if is false. Wait — the else branch body is the second message, which will run whenever check_flag was VALID (first condition false). That means: any call with a valid CONTAINS/EQ flag → goes to else → fatal \"unknown stream type\"! Broken. And when check_flag invalid → first fatal. So it ALWAYS fails essentially. So validation is a bug: they conflated two independent checks into if/else. Should be two separate if/endif blocks, not if/else. Also `else(<condition>)` is not `elseif(<condition>)`.\n\nAlso referencing `${check_flag}` unquoted in comparisons: `if(NOT ${check_flag} STREQUAL ...)`. If check_flag is e.g. \"CONTAINS\", expands to `if(NOT CONTAINS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\" AND NOT CONTAINS STREQUAL \"EQ\")`. Hmm, when a bareword in if matches an undefined var, treated as string \"CONTAINS\". `NOT CONTAINS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\"` — operator precedence: NOT applies to `CONTAINS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\"`? Actually `if(NOT <cond>)`. And AND splits. The token after NOT: `CONTAINS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\"` → this is `CONTAINS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\"` — is CONTAINS treated as a variable name? If var undefined → empty string → empty STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\" false → NOT false = true. So behaves as intended-ish but fragile. Better quote: `if(NOT \"${check_flag}\" STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\" AND NOT \"${check_flag}\" STREQUAL \"EQ\")`. Since CONTAINS could be undefined? It is a parameter value from caller = literal string \"CONTAINS\"; in if() an undefined variable name resolves to empty — but here the token is `CONTAINS` which is also... hmm ambiguity: if there's a variable named CONTAINS not defined, treated as literal \"CONTAINS\"? CMake if: a quoted string is literal. An unquoted token is evaluated as a variable if one with that name exists, else treated as literal string constant. So `CONTAINS` → literal. So `if(NOT CONTAINS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\")`:  token CONTAINS literal equals \"CONTAINS\"? Actually STREQUAL compares strings; left CONTAINS literal \"CONTAINS\", right \"CONTAINS\" → equal → NOT equal = false → then AND second NOT CONTAINS STREQUAL \"EQ\" → true... false AND true = false → skip message. Good for valid. So currently the logic is subtly right only for valid flags but it's fragile style, plus the else bug.\n\nActually wait if check_flag invalid e.g. \"BOGUS\": NOT BOGUS STREQUAL \"CONTAINS\" → true; second NOT BOGUS STREQUAL \"EQ\" → true; true AND true → message fatal unknown flag. Good. Then valid CONTAINS → if false → else → runs second message unknown stream — ALWAYS fatal when flag valid! So any normal call breaks. Broken.\n\nThen the later blocks at L44-50 and L53-57 do re-test stream and check_flag with separate if/elseif but no else, and they duplicate the fatal logic poorly.\n\nActually given the early fatal validation is broken, the later if/elseif (L44, L53) without else fall through fine if values valid.\n\n3. The validation of stream uses `${stream} EQUAL 1` — comparing numbers ok, but referencing unquoted; stream=2 number fine.\n\n4. `PROGRAMM_RESULT` typo (double M) — cosmetic; used consistently within function though. Not an error, just name.\n\n5. The add_test for run_case passes `\"${args}\"` — quoted empty string as a single argv element? In add_test, each list item becomes separate argv. `\"${args}\"` where args=\"\" yields empty string element → becomes an empty argument. run_case argv after `--`: `$<TARGET_FILE:t1_c>`, then empty string. In run_case the exe is argv[6], and args from argv[7]. Empty args element → cmdline gets appended ' ' + \"\"? Let's see run_case: cmdline built with loop i=7..argc-1, appending ' ' + argv[i]. Empty argv[i] appends just a space → trailing space, harmless. But adds an empty argument to child command line? Actually CreateProcessA parses command line; a trailing space does nothing. It may produce empty argv for child? The command string would be `\"path\" ` (path + space). Fine. But passing \"\" as an actual argv element to run_case means argc counts it. run_case's own loop adds argv[i] i.e., empty → cmdline += ' ' then += \"\" → OK harmless. But the TODO L41 flags it.\n\n6. Bigger structural issue: The call to `add_case` is in the ROOT CMakeLists.txt AFTER `add_subdirectory(tests)`. Function `add_case` is defined inside tests/CMakeLists.txt (child scope). Functions defined in a subdirectory are NOT visible in the parent scope after add_subdirectory returns! Actually functions defined with function() are directory-scoped? Functions are commands; when add_subdirectory processes tests/CMakeLists, function add_case defined within that directory scope. After returning to root scope, is add_case still defined? CMake functions defined in a subdirectory do not leak up to the parent directory. So calling add_case at root L132 → \"Unknown CMake command add_case\". \n\nWait, is that right? Function definitions in CMake behave like a kind of scope: a function defined in a subdirectory is available in that directory and its subdirectories, but NOT in the parent directory. Yes — \"Command ... is not available in parent scope\" I believe macros and functions defined in a subdir are not available after add_subdirectory returns. Actually let me think: variables set in subdir do not propagate up. Function/macro definitions — CMake docs: \"A function or macro defined in a subdirectory is not available in the parent.\" Hmm I think functions are processed as command definitions stored per-directory. Let me recall examples: People commonly define a function in the top-level CMakeLists before add_subdirectory to reuse in children. Defining in child and using in parent after return — not available. So this call at root L132 referencing add_case defined in tests/ is a bug: unknown command error.\n\nGiven the tests currently: the old manual add_test in root L132-141 commented out (#[[ ]] block). The add_case call at L132 uncommented. So running cmake now would: enable_testing, ad