ually the real difference between if/elseif and two ifs here:\n- if/elseif: if flag bad → fatal. Only reached stream check when flag is good.\n- two ifs: same behavior actually because of FATAL_ERROR stopping.\n\nThe real honest difference: if we wanted to report both errors at once (accumulate), we'd need message() not fatal, or collect errors. With FATAL_ERROR both approaches stop at first error. So the actual semantic difference is minimal — my original phrasing was muddled and confused her.\n\nLet me clarify honestly: I conflated. With FATAL_ERROR, both behave the same when both are bad — first fatal wins. The difference between if/elseif and two ifs is purely about structure/readability, not runtime behavior in the fatal case.\n\nActually wait, there IS a subtle difference. Consider:\n- if/elseif: stream check runs only if flag check passed. So if flag bad → reports flag. If flag good, stream bad → reports stream.\n- two ifs: flag check; if bad fatal. Otherwise stream check; if bad fatal. Same outcomes.\n\nSo with FATAL_ERROR the observable behavior is identical. The only real difference would be if we used non-fatal message + return, or wanted to run both checks without stopping.\n\nSo I should just honestly say: my sentence was poorly worded. Both approaches behave identically here because FATAL_ERROR stops at the first failure. The two-if structure is purely about readability (\"these are two separate concerns\"), not about reporting both errors. If I implied two ifs report both errors — that's wrong, neither does.\n\nLet me give a clean explanation.\n\nKeep it concise, in Russian, plan mode read-only, no tools needed.",
